Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:20:25 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 14/14] macOS: Level Zero sysman frequency range control and engine
 activity over IntelXe.kext

Follow-up to the sysman patch (0013), for a kext with sensors_raw
version 2 (live SLPC range control and the GuC's engine activity
statistics):

- zesFrequencySetRange: the device's freq0/min_freq and max_freq are
  writable as on Linux (0644 when the process may set the range:
  root and a kext with live range control, xemac_freq_can_set; else
  0444). A write of a number there sets the kext's
  debug.intelxe.slpc_min / slpc_max (xemac_freq_set), which the kext
  clamps to RPn..RP0 and sends to the GuC at once, so sysman's Linux
  frequency code (setMin/setMax) runs unchanged. A non-root open for
  writing fails with EACCES (INSUFFICIENT_PERMISSIONS, as on Linux);
  anything but an unsigned number is EINVAL, as xe's min_freq_store.
- canControl: on macOS also whether min_freq and max_freq can be
  written (FsAccess::canWrite: the mode above), so it reflects
  root-or-not instead of every SetRange failing.
- zesEngineGetActivity: Linux sysman opens xe's PMU events
  engine-active-ticks / engine-total-ticks with perf_event_open and
  reads them as a group. The tree now has that PMU's sysfs description
  (/sys/devices/xe_<bdf>/type, events/, format/ as xe_pmu.c registers
  it) when the kext reports engine counters, and
  PmuInterfaceImp::perfEventOpen on macOS opens an emulated event
  (MacSysfs::perfEventOpen): read() answers as perf does for a group
  (nr, time_enabled, values), the values the kext's per-engine GuC
  tick counters from one sample (a count never goes back; an engine
  missing from a sample, the media GT while it is down, keeps its
  last). readFunction goes through NEO::SysCalls::read on macOS.
  Sysman's engine module, the xe KMD interface (configs, group reads,
  the per-group averaging) run unchanged; handles per engine and per
  group as on Linux.

NEO_MACOS_SYSFS_TRACE=1 also logs the writes and the event opens.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
